  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "A differential equation links a function to its derivatives; solving one means recovering the family of curves that fit.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Reference — Separable equations",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Slope fields</strong> sketch solution curves. A <strong>separable</strong> equation \\( \\dfrac{dy}{dx}=g(x)h(y) \\) solves as \\( \\displaystyle\\int\\frac{dy}{h(y)}=\\int g(x)\\,dx \\). Exponential change obeys \\( y=y_0e^{kt} \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "figure",
        "figure": {
          "type": "slope_field",
          "title": "Slope Field for dy/dx = x - y",
          "fn": "x - y",
          "xrange": [-3, 3],
          "yrange": [-3, 3],
          "solution": { "x0": 0, "y0": 1 }
        },
        "caption": {
          "en": "Slope field for $\\frac{dy}{dx} = x - y$ with particular solution curve through $(0,1)$. Each segment depicts the local derivative direction $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at that grid point.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Separable",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{dy}{h(y)}=\\int g\\,dx \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Exponential",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( y=y_0e^{kt} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Slope field",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "plot \\( y' \\) directions",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Particular",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "fix \\( C \\) from a point",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 7.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Solve \\( \\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2y \\) with \\( y(0)=3 \\).</p>\\[ y=3e^{2x}. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example — Slope field &amp; separation",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Solve \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2xy\\) with \\(y(0)=3\\).</p>\\[\\frac{dy}{y}=2x\\,dx\\;\\Rightarrow\\;\\ln|y|=x^2+C\\;\\Rightarrow\\;y=Ae^{x^2}.\\]<p>Initial condition: \\(3=Ae^0=A\\). Solution: \\(y=3e^{x^2}\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠ Common traps — Separating variables & the initial constant +C",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Separation must be multiplicative:</strong> An equation is separable only if it can be written as \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = g(x)h(y) \\). An expression like \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = x - y \\) cannot be separated by adding or subtracting terms across sides.</p><p><strong>Always write \\( +C \\) on the integration step:</strong> Writing \\( \\ln|y| = x^2 + C \\) at the exact moment of anti-differentiation is mandatory. On AP FRQ scoring guidelines, omitting \\( +C \\) here caps the question at at most 1 point out of 5 or 6!</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0\"><strong>Exponents and the constant:</strong> \\( \\ln|y| = x^2 + C \\implies |y| = e^{x^2 + C} = e^C e^{x^2} = A e^{x^2} \\). Writing \\( y = e^{x^2} + C \\) is a fatal algebraic error.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "A population grows at a rate proportional to its size. If \\(P(0)=500\\) and \\(P(2)=800\\), find \\(P(t)\\) and determine when \\(P=2000\\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "\\(P=500e^{kt}\\). \\(800=500e^{2k}\\Rightarrow k=\\frac{\\ln(8/5)}{2}\\approx0.2350\\). \\(2000=500e^{kt}\\Rightarrow t=\\frac{\\ln4}{k}\\approx5.90\\) years.",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
